<div style="width: 100%; padding: 15px; background-color: #e0f2f7; border: 2px solid #a7d9eb; text-align: center;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # 2 – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
    </div>

    <table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
      <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
      </tr>
      <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 8th Grade (CEFR: A2.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Technology</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Pros and Cons</td>
      </tr>
      <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td colspan="3" style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
      </tr>
      <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td colspan="3" style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
      </tr>
      <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td colspan="3" style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
          <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Comprehend descriptions in simple texts. Reading: Analyze informational texts for specific purposes. Phonemic Awareness: Demonstrate knowledge of phonemic awareness by pronouncing the past tense verbs ending "ed."</p>
          <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can comprehend descriptions in simple texts about technology use (e.g., "How does technology help us in school?").
    Can analyze informational texts to understand the pros and cons of technology (e.g., "Digital technology makes communication faster and easier, but it can be distracting." "List two benefits of using technology.").
    Can identify and pronounce past tense verbs in short texts, distinguishing between /d/, /ɪd/, and /t/ sounds (e.g., /d/ in "played," /ɪd/ in "started," and /t/ in "worked").</p>
        </td>
      </tr>
    </table>

    <h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; margin-top: 20px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
    <table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
      <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
          <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 35%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
          <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 45%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
          <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 20%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
          <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
          </td>
          <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays images of people using different types of technology (e.g., a student using a laptop for homework, someone talking on a cellphone, friends interacting on social media, a family watching TV). Students work in pairs to discuss: "What are these people doing with technology? Is it helpful or distracting?" (5 min)</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to quickly scan a text for keywords related to pros and cons, using a short example paragraph. "When we read, we can look for words like 'benefits' or 'problems' to understand the main idea." The teacher also models the correct pronunciation of past tense verbs ending "-ed" from a projected list (e.g., "saved" /d/, "liked" /t/, "started" /ɪd/). (5 min)</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of the warm-up discussion and the modeling.
            <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What are some benefits of technology we discussed?" (Elicit answers like 'helps us learn', 'faster communication'.)</li>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What does 'distracting' mean when we talk about technology?" (Guide to 'takes your focus away'.)</li>
            </ul>
            </p>
          </td>
          <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___ / ___</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(10 min)</p>
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
          <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Technology".</p>
          </td>
          <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Students receive a short informational article (approx. 150-200 words) titled "Technology: A Double-Edged Sword for Teens." The text describes various ways teenagers use technology, focusing on the pros and cons (e.g., "Digital technology makes communication faster and easier, but it can be distracting." "Social media can help you feel connected, but it can also reduce meaningful interactions face-to-face." "Some teens have found a solution to overuse by choosing to disconnect more often."). The article includes target vocabulary (e.g., technology, cellphone, internet, device, social media, solution, benefits, use, reduce, disconnect, interact, save, helpful, meaningful, connected, relaxed, distracted, focused, often, usually, actively) and grammatical structures (modals: can, should; comparatives: faster, easier; present perfect: "I have spent more time... since I stopped..."). It also contains several past tense verbs ending in "-ed" (e.g., "created," "connected," "enjoyed," "started," "watched," "used"). (5 min)</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Reading: Comprehend descriptions in simple texts about technology use; Analyze informational texts for specific purposes.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
            <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students read the title and first paragraph. Then, answer a gist question: "What is the main idea of this article?" (Students choose from multiple choice options or summarize in one sentence).</li>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students read the entire text once. Then, complete True/False statements based on the general content, e.g., "True or False: The article says technology always makes people feel relaxed." (Focus on initial overall comprehension).</li>
            </ul>
          </td>
          <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___ / ___</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(10 min)</p>
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
          <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
          </td>
          <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Comprehension of modals, comparatives, present perfect, and target vocabulary in context. Identifying and pronouncing past tense verbs ending "-ed." Pragmatic functions: Discussing pros and cons.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
            <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 1 (Controlled Practice – Cloze & Identification):</b> Students re-read the article carefully. First, they complete sentences with target vocabulary words (e.g., "Digital _______ makes communication faster and easier." "Spending too much time on your _______ can make you feel distracted.") by selecting from a word bank. Second, they identify all sentences containing modals ("can," "could," "should"), comparatives ("easier," "more difficult"), and present perfect ("have spent... since") from the text. They underline these structures. (7 min)</li>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 2 (Semi-controlled – Categorization & Pronunciation):</b> In groups of three, students create two lists from the text: "Pros of Technology" and "Cons of Technology." They must use complete sentences from the article (e.g., "One benefit is that technology makes communication faster and easier." "A con is that social media can be distracting."). While doing this, they also identify all past tense verbs ending in "-ed" in the text. The teacher then writes a few of these verbs on the board (e.g., "created," "connected," "enjoyed," "started," "watched," "used") and leads a brief choral and individual practice of their /d/, /ɪd/, and /t/ sounds. (8 min)</li>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 3 (Pair Interaction – Peer Check & Discussion):</b> Students compare their "Pros and Cons" lists from Activity 2 with another pair. They check for accuracy and completeness based on the text. Then, they discuss: "Which pro or con from the text do you think is most important for teenagers today?" and "Could you give a suggestion to reduce the negative effects of technology, like 'You could try using less screen time'?" (This practices giving opinions and offering suggestions). They also take turns reading sentences from the text that contain past tense -ed verbs, providing peer feedback on pronunciation. (5 min)</li>
            </ul>
          </td>
          <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___ / ___</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(20 min)</p>
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
          <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Technology".</p>
          </td>
          <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Infographic Design.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students, in their groups, will design a simple infographic (on a large sheet of paper or digital tool) summarizing the "Pros and Cons of Technology for Teens" based *solely* on the provided reading text. They must include:
            <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">At least three "pros" and three "cons" identified from the article.</li>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">A section suggesting at least one "solution" (e.g., "You could try to disconnect more often") based on information or implications from the text.</li>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They should use key vocabulary (e.g., benefits, distracted, connected, solution) and grammatical structures (e.g., "Technology makes communication easier," "We should reduce screen time") extracted or adapted from the text.</li>
            </ul>
            The focus is on their ability to analyze and synthesize information from the reading. (10 min)</p>
          </td>
          <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___ / ___</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(10 min)</p>
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
          <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
          </td>
          <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation during group work and a quick peer-assessment of the infographics.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each group presents their infographic to another group, briefly explaining one pro, one con, and one suggestion. While presenting, they must read aloud one sentence from their infographic that contains a past tense verb ending in "-ed." The listening group provides peer feedback using a simple checklist.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
            <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accuracy of information from the text (Are the pros/cons actually in the article?).</li>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target vocabulary (e.g., benefits, distracted, solution) from the lesson.</li>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of presentation and organization of infographic.</li>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of the selected past tense verb ending in "-ed" (teacher focuses on /d/, /ɪd/, /t/ distinction).</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a brief exit ticket before leaving:
            <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"List one new thing you learned about technology from today's reading."</li>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of the article was easiest/most difficult to understand?"</li>
            </ul>
            </p>
          </td>
          <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___ / ___</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(8 min)</p>
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
          <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
          </td>
          <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
            <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How well do you think you can now identify the pros and cons of technology from a text?"</li>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What strategy helped you the most today to understand the reading?"</li>
            </ul>
            </p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The reading text was appropriate for the A2.3 level, and students generally grasped the main ideas. The categorization of pros and cons worked well for comprehension. More targeted practice might be needed for distinguishing the three "-ed" sounds in real-time reading, perhaps with a quick warm-up drill next lesson. The infographic task successfully encouraged analysis of the text.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> In Lesson 3, students will move from reading about the pros and cons to actively speaking and interacting about them. The vocabulary and understanding of the topic developed in this reading lesson will be directly applied in communicative speaking tasks and discussions.</p>
          </td>
          <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___ / ___</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(2 min)</p>
          </td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>